"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_4_m_1_d_0_n=self.webpackChunk_4_m_1_d_0_n||[]).push([[3518],{34369:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab","metadata":{"permalink":"/deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab","source":"@site/blog/2025-05-12-Deploying a Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox.mdx","title":"Deploying a  Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox","description":"Step-by-step guide to installing and configuring pfSense as the core router/firewall, Splunk as the SIEM in a segmented cybersecurity lab running on VirtualBox.","date":"2025-05-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"cybersecurity","permalink":"/tags/cybersecurity"},{"inline":true,"label":"homelab","permalink":"/tags/homelab"},{"inline":true,"label":"virtualbox","permalink":"/tags/virtualbox"},{"inline":true,"label":"pfSense","permalink":"/tags/pf-sense"},{"inline":true,"label":"network-segmentation","permalink":"/tags/network-segmentation"},{"inline":true,"label":"lab-setup","permalink":"/tags/lab-setup"}],"readingTime":4.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Deploying a  Cybersecurity Lab on VirtualBox","description":"Step-by-step guide to installing and configuring pfSense as the core router/firewall, Splunk as the SIEM in a segmented cybersecurity lab running on VirtualBox.","slug":"deploy-virtualbox-cyber-lab","tags":["cybersecurity","homelab","virtualbox","pfSense","network-segmentation","lab-setup"],"hide_table_of_contents":false},"unlisted":false},"content":":::warning\\n**This post is a work in progress \u2014 updates coming soon!** \\n:::\\n\\n\\nThis blog kicks off a series dedicated to building a complete **cybersecurity home lab** on VirtualBox \u2014 designed to support both **red teaming** and **blue teaming** activities. From attacking vulnerable services to detecting malicious behavior with a SIEM, this lab provides a self-contained environment to practice real-world scenarios.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n\\nThroughout this project, we deploy and configure:\\n- **Kali Linux**: our attacker and administration machine  \\n- **pfSense**: a firewall/router to segment our lab into realistic network zones  \\n- **GOAD (Active Directory lab)**: to simulate internal corporate infrastructure  \\n- **A vulnerable web server in the DMZ**  \\n- **Splunk** (or ELK): for centralized log collection and detection engineering  \\n- **OpenVAS/Nessus**: to simulate vulnerability scans and management\\n\\nEach system is deployed on VirtualBox and connected through isolated internal networks, allowing realistic simulation of internal, external, and DMZ scenarios. The lab is entirely **offline and open-source friendly** (except where noted, like Nessus Essentials).\\n\\nThis post gives a full architectural overview and deployment philosophy, with links to step-by-step guides for each component.\\n\\n\\nimport React from \'react\';\\n\\n## My Network Architecture\\n\\nHere is the diagram of the VirtualBox network setup:\\n\\n<div style={{position: \\"relative\\", width: \\"100%\\", height: \\"500px\\"}}>\\n  <iframe\\n    className=\\"responsive-iframe\\"\\n    frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n    style={{\\n      position: \\"absolute\\",\\n      top: 0,\\n      left: 0,\\n      width: \\"100%\\",\\n      height: \\"100%\\"\\n    }}\\n    loading=\\"lazy\\"\\n    src=\\"https://viewer.diagrams.net/?tags=%7B%7D&highlight=0000ff&layers=1&nav=1&title=MyDiagram#Uhttps%3A%2F%2Fdrive.google.com%2Fuc%3Fid%3D1dJP_nJqBZliPa_Dum1ZIDOIQa0hSF8CH%26export%3Ddownload\\"\\n  ></iframe>\\n</div>\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n# Building a VirtualBox-Based Cybersecurity Home Lab\\n\\nSetting up a dedicated cybersecurity lab is one of the most effective ways to sharpen your skills, experiment safely, and document your progress. Whether you\'re preparing for certifications, building a red/blue team mindset, or simply exploring new tools, having your own lab environment offers freedom and depth unmatched by online platforms.\\n\\nIn this blog series, I document the complete setup of my **VirtualBox-based cyber range**, focused on **pentesting, blue teaming (SOC), and vulnerability operations (VOC)**. Everything is hosted locally and uses **free, open-source tools**, making it highly accessible and reproducible.\\n\\n> \ud83d\udca1 If you\'re just looking to jump straight into configuring a core component, check out:\\n> [Building a Security Home Lab in VirtualBox](/docs/category/building-a-security-home-lab-in-virtualbox)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Why Build a Home Lab?\\n\\nThere are tons of platforms out there (HTB, TryHackMe, etc.), but building your own lab helps you:\\n\\n* Develop **practical skills** in real-world architectures.\\n* Learn how **network segmentation**, **routing**, and **firewalling** work.\\n* Create controlled environments to test malware, exploits, detections, and defenses.\\n* Practice the **attack\u2013detect\u2013defend** triad used in many professional environments.\\n* Gain hands-on exposure with **SIEM**, **vulnerability scanners**, **Active Directory**, and **DMZ setups**.\\n\\nThis lab is designed to mirror enterprise-grade complexity while staying manageable for a single host machine using VirtualBox.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Lab Architecture Overview\\n\\nHere\'s a high-level view of the lab\u2019s architecture:\\n\\n| Zone                         | Description                                                | Tools/VMs                                   |\\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\\n| **Internal Network**      | Simulates a corporate LAN.                                 | GOAD (Active Directory), workstations       |\\n| **DMZ**                   | Exposed web server to simulate public-facing applications. | Vulnerable Linux web app                    |\\n| **Attacker Zone**         | Where attacks are launched and tests are run.              | Kali Linux                                  |\\n| **Routing/Firewall**      | Controls traffic between zones.                            | pfSense                                     |\\n| **SOC (Monitoring)**      | Central log aggregation and detection.                     | ELK Stack (ElasticSearch, Logstash, Kibana) |\\n| **VOC (Vuln Management)** | Detect and manage vulnerabilities.                         | OpenVAS or similar                          |\\n\\nAll virtual machines are interconnected through VirtualBox\'s internal networks, with pfSense acting as the router and firewall between zones. Each network is segmented, offering a realistic testbed for attacks and defenses.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Project Structure\\n\\nThe full deployment is structured in **stages**, with each post documenting:\\n\\n* The **deployment** of a specific component.\\n* Key **configuration steps**.\\n* How each component fits into the bigger picture.\\n* Preparation for a final **Attack / Detect / Defend** demo with CVEs.\\n\\n### Deployment Roadmap:\\n\\n1. Install and configure pfSense with all necessary interfaces\\n2. Install Kali Linux as the attack/admin machine\\n3. Configure pfSense firewall rules\\n4. Deploy a vulnerable Linux web server in the DMZ\\n5. Install GOAD (Active Directory simulation)\\n6. Install and configure the ELK stack for log centralization\\n7. Set up log forwarders and visibility tools\\n8. Deploy OpenVAS for vulnerability scanning\\n9. Launch real-world CVE exploits, detect them via logs, and respond using blue team techniques\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Tools and Technologies\\n\\nThis lab uses only **free, open-source** tools:\\n\\n* **VirtualBox** (VMs)\\n* **pfSense** (firewall/router)\\n* **Kali Linux** (attacker)\\n* **GOAD** (Active Directory lab)\\n* **Ubuntu/Debian** (vulnerable apps, web server)\\n* **ELK Stack** (SIEM)\\n* **OpenVAS** (VOC)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Who Is This For?\\n\\nThis series is designed for:\\n\\n* Security enthusiasts wanting to go beyond CTFs.\\n* Pentesters looking to simulate internal/external assessments.\\n* Blue teamers and SOC analysts aiming to improve detection engineering.\\n* Students prepping for certs like OSCP, CPTS, or Blue Team certs.\\n* Anyone building a portfolio to land a first job in cybersecurity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Stay Tuned\\n\\nEach new component comes with a full walkthrough and technical write-up. You\u2019ll find detailed steps, explanations, and screenshots.\\n\\nBy the end of this series, you\'ll have a fully functional lab that supports both red and blue team exercises.\\n\\nHappy hacking & defending!"}]}}')}}]);